-- title:
--   Lattices $\mathcal L(B)$, cosets $2\mathcal L+w$ and closest vectors in the $\ell_p$ norm
-- statement:
--   For a matrix $B\in\mathbb R^{m\times k}$ the **lattice** generated by its columns is
--
--   $$\mathcal L(B)=B\mathbb Z^k=\{Bz: z\in\mathbb Z^k\},$$
--
--   and for $w\in\mathbb R^m$ the **coset** of $2\mathcal L$ through $w$ is $2\mathcal L+w=\{2Bz+w: z\in\mathbb Z^k\}$. With $\|x\|_p^p=\sum_j |x_j|^p$ for $x\in\mathbb R^m$ and $p\ge1$, a vector $v$ is a **closest vector** of $\mathcal L(B)$ to a target $t$ in the $\ell_p$ norm if $v\in\mathcal L(B)$ and $\|v-t\|_p\le\|y-t\|_p$ for every $y\in\mathcal L(B)$. For a rational basis $B\in\mathbb Q^{n\times n}$ and a rational target, the same notion is read in $\mathbb R^n$; this is the output condition of the problem $\mathrm{CVP}_p$.
--
--   These are the objects of every statement of the mission: the goal theorem asks for a closest vector, and the analysis of the algorithm works inside a coset of $2\mathcal L$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The set $\mathcal L(B)$ is defined for every matrix; statements that need a basis assume linearly independent columns (or $\det B\neq0$ for a square basis). Closeness is compared through $\|\cdot\|_p^p$, which is equivalent for $p>0$. The membership $v\in\mathcal L(B)$ is part of the definition: without it the target itself would be "closest". The norms $\|\cdot\|_p^p$, $\|\cdot\|_p$, $\|\cdot\|_2$ are the published $\texttt{PNormReg.lpPow}$, $\texttt{lpNorm}$, $\texttt{l2Norm}$.
-- source:
--   Aggarwal, Morolia, How to Round Spiky Balls into Submission; And Faster Algorithms For The Closest Vector Problem, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 535c3bcc…), §2.1, p.10 (PDF p.12); §2.3, p.14 (PDF p.16); Definition 2.9, p.15 (PDF p.17); Fact 2.7, p.14

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_PNormReg_Norms

open PNormReg

namespace SpikyCVP

/-- The lattice `L(B) := BZ^k` generated by the columns of `B ∈ ℝ^{m×k}`.
Aggarwal, Morolia, *How to Round Spiky Balls into Submission; And Faster Algorithms For The Closest
Vector Problem*, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 535c3bcc…), §2.3, p.14 (PDF p.16): "A lattice
of rank k in Rⁿ is a set L = L(B) := BZ^k, where B ∈ R^{n×k} has linearly independent columns".

**Formalization Note.** The set is defined for every matrix; each statement that needs a basis carries
the independence of the columns (or `det B ≠ 0` for a square basis) as a hypothesis. -/
def latt {m k : ℕ} (B : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin k) ℝ) : Set (Fin m → ℝ) :=
  {x | ∃ z : Fin k → ℤ, x = B.mulVec (fun i => (z i : ℝ))}

/-- The coset `2L + w = {2Bz + w : z ∈ Z^k}` of `2L` (Fact 2.7, p.14; the coset `M = 2L + v∗ − t` of §4,
p.18). -/
def twoLPlus {m k : ℕ} (B : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin k) ℝ) (w : Fin m → ℝ) : Set (Fin m → ℝ) :=
  {y | ∃ z : Fin k → ℤ, y = (2 : ℝ) • B.mulVec (fun i => (z i : ℝ)) + w}

/-- `v` is a closest vector of `L(B)` to `t` in the `ℓp` norm (§2.3, p.14: "A vector x ∈ L with
∥x − t∥p = distp(L, t) is a closest vector to t"; Definition 2.9, p.15).

**Formalization Note.** The membership `v ∈ L(B)` is part of the definition: without it `v = t` would be
"closest". Distances are compared through `∥·∥p^p = Σ_j |x_j|^p` (the published `PNormReg.lpPow`), which is
equivalent for `p > 0`. -/
def IsClosestVec {m k : ℕ} (p : ℝ) (B : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin k) ℝ) (t v : Fin m → ℝ) : Prop :=
  v ∈ latt B ∧ ∀ y ∈ latt B, lpPow p (v - t) ≤ lpPow p (y - t)

/-- The rational instance of `CVPp` (Definition 2.9, p.15): for a basis `B ∈ Q^{n×n}` and a target
`t ∈ Qⁿ`, the rational vector `x` is a closest vector of `L(B)` to `t` in the `ℓp` norm, everything read
in `ℝ`. -/
def IsClosest {n : ℕ} (p : ℚ) (B : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℚ) (t x : Fin n → ℚ) : Prop :=
  IsClosestVec (p : ℝ) (B.map fun q : ℚ => (q : ℝ)) (fun j => (t j : ℝ)) (fun j => (x j : ℝ))

end SpikyCVP


